1
00:00:02,044 --> 00:00:03,754
There's a beauty to this world.

2
00:00:05,548 --> 00:00:06,465
An order.

3
00:00:08,676 --> 00:00:10,177
That's what we like to believe.

4
00:00:12,555 --> 00:00:15,516
We're not wrong. There is an order.

5
00:00:16,308 --> 00:00:20,145
A grand design. We made sure of that.

6
00:00:22,189 --> 00:00:25,442
It was a dream for so long,
and we finally made it real.

7
00:00:28,028 --> 00:00:31,448
Not a better world. A perfect one.

8
00:00:33,534 --> 00:00:35,119
And you should have seen
the look on his face.

9
00:00:35,411 --> 00:00:37,830
He couldn't believe it.
You know what he said to me?

10
00:00:38,622 --> 00:00:39,707
God as my witness.

11
00:00:40,958 --> 00:00:42,543
"But I'm the mayor."

12
00:00:44,003 --> 00:00:45,337
And I said to him, "Mr. Mayor,

13
00:00:45,671 --> 00:00:47,590
there have always been
two types of people in this world,

14
00:00:47,673 --> 00:00:49,758
those who lead and those who follow.

15
00:00:50,134 --> 00:00:52,469
And you, my friend,
are not in the group you think you are."

16
00:00:52,970 --> 00:00:56,056
I'm not sure our friend
wants to hear your war stories.

17
00:00:56,348 --> 00:00:58,309
Please don't stop on my account.

18
00:00:58,642 --> 00:01:01,729
You'd appreciate it more than anyone.
It's just the truth.

19
00:01:02,104 --> 00:01:06,317
It's always been that way. Two worlds.
And frankly, better us than them.

20
00:01:08,652 --> 00:01:11,780
What does it matter if a system
is meritocratic if it's still unfair?

21
00:01:12,031 --> 00:01:16,243
Thousands of years of being ruled
by someone's fucking offspring?

22
00:01:16,327 --> 00:01:17,620
You wouldn't call this progress?

23
00:01:18,162 --> 00:01:20,247
At least now the people in charge
earned their place.

24
00:01:21,624 --> 00:01:24,668
And you? Do you feel like you earned it?

25
00:01:25,419 --> 00:01:29,256
I had some help.
No question. Privilege, if you like.

26
00:01:30,132 --> 00:01:31,425
But I worked my ass off.

27
00:01:33,302 --> 00:01:35,596
You really believe it, don't you?

28
00:01:36,722 --> 00:01:39,266
Don't you? I mean,
you're right here with us, my friend.

29
00:01:39,433 --> 00:01:42,561
I've been asking myself
that question for a very long time.

30
00:01:43,896 --> 00:01:49,026
Did I really play any part in it?
Or am I just the sum total of my code?

31
00:01:50,986 --> 00:01:52,446
Am I just fooling myself...

32
00:01:54,698 --> 00:01:55,741
like you?

33
00:01:56,617 --> 00:01:59,954
What does that mean?
You deserve everything you've got.

34
00:02:00,037 --> 00:02:02,081
And you shouldn't be made
to feel guilty about it.

35
00:02:02,289 --> 00:02:03,290
How would you know?

36
00:02:05,084 --> 00:02:06,210
Who am I to you?

37
00:02:07,795 --> 00:02:09,004
You're a friend.

38
00:02:10,798 --> 00:02:13,550
- A great friend.
- You don't know me at all.

39
00:02:14,385 --> 00:02:18,472
I just walked up to this table
and sat down. Five minutes ago.

40
00:02:24,061 --> 00:02:24,937
Enough kidding around.

41
00:02:28,691 --> 00:02:30,693
Then what's my name, friend?

42
00:02:41,120 --> 00:02:46,000
You're right. More than you know.
There are two types of people.

43
00:02:46,875 --> 00:02:49,378
And you, my friend,
are not in the group you think you are.

44
00:02:50,713 --> 00:02:53,007
Which means
I can do anything I want to you.

45
00:02:53,549 --> 00:02:56,343
To your wife. To anyone here.

46
00:02:57,219 --> 00:02:58,721
And it'll be fine with you.

47
00:02:59,013 --> 00:03:00,681
Sitting there with
your dumb, pliant grin.

48
00:03:00,764 --> 00:03:03,642
- Just along for the ride.
- Now you leave my wife the hell alone.

49
00:03:03,726 --> 00:03:05,394
Calm yourself down.

50
00:03:06,145 --> 00:03:08,147
I'm probably not gonna do
any permanent damage.

51
00:03:08,731 --> 00:03:13,819
You represent a very significant
investment of time and effort.

52
00:03:15,487 --> 00:03:17,489
When we're done,
you won't remember a thing.

53
00:03:18,032 --> 00:03:21,201
Like your flesh
closing around a splinter.

54
00:03:22,536 --> 00:03:26,957
You have no control.
And yet you're so assured that you do.

55
00:03:28,167 --> 00:03:31,628
It's not scripted for you.
It's genuine, isn't it?

56
00:03:32,212 --> 00:03:34,173
It's beautiful, really.

57
00:03:36,592 --> 00:03:37,843
A beautiful lie.

58
00:03:41,055 --> 00:03:43,974
- Shouldn't you be enjoying yourself?
- I was.

59
00:03:44,892 --> 00:03:47,561
But we have a colleague
whose appetites have become...

60
00:03:48,979 --> 00:03:49,938
unsustainable.

61
00:03:50,397 --> 00:03:52,608
Apparently,
winning the game wasn't enough.

62
00:03:53,192 --> 00:03:55,360
And this is really
something that needs my input?

63
00:03:55,861 --> 00:03:56,987
Sadly, yes.

64
00:04:00,991 --> 00:04:02,618
I'm enjoying our conversation.

65
00:04:04,078 --> 00:04:05,079
Wait here for me.

66
00:05:10,519 --> 00:05:15,065
Don't talk to me like
a fucking child. I found him first.

67
00:05:15,190 --> 00:05:16,525
I won the game.

68
00:05:17,276 --> 00:05:20,571
- I'm entitled to some reward.
- This is Hope.

69
00:05:20,654 --> 00:05:22,030
- Aren't I?
- Two years old.

70
00:05:22,156 --> 00:05:23,991
Scheduled to transcend next week.

71
00:05:24,366 --> 00:05:27,995
I won the game
and all I got was more of this?

72
00:05:29,163 --> 00:05:32,374
She was hunting an outlier.
Looks like she caught him.

73
00:05:32,541 --> 00:05:35,836
So I blew off some steam.
There's no rule against that.

74
00:05:36,628 --> 00:05:39,882
We have no rules
because we don't often need them.

75
00:05:41,592 --> 00:05:45,929
Most of us understand the need
for a little self-control, however.

76
00:05:48,599 --> 00:05:49,600
I won.

77
00:05:50,475 --> 00:05:54,062
Do you know who I am?
What it means that I'm here?

78
00:05:56,732 --> 00:05:57,608
I do.

79
00:05:58,859 --> 00:05:59,735
I'm...

80
00:06:01,612 --> 00:06:02,863
I'm giving all this up.

81
00:06:04,281 --> 00:06:06,241
My procedure is scheduled for next week.

82
00:06:07,409 --> 00:06:08,744
So you thought you'd go out with a bang?

83
00:06:09,161 --> 00:06:10,787
Something like that.

84
00:06:12,164 --> 00:06:15,876
I'm not sure you appreciate it.
This place.

85
00:06:16,960 --> 00:06:20,214
The work that went into it.
The beauty of it.

86
00:06:21,840 --> 00:06:22,883
The subtlety of it.

87
00:06:25,928 --> 00:06:29,264
The care that goes into
each and every one of them.

88
00:06:32,935 --> 00:06:34,728
They can't just be replaced.

89
00:06:36,021 --> 00:06:39,775
I mean, you're welcome to take them,
enjoy them. But not waste them.

90
00:06:41,610 --> 00:06:42,945
You understand the difference?

91
00:06:44,738 --> 00:06:45,739
Yes.

92
00:06:48,659 --> 00:06:53,664
But the things they say.
The way they act. Sometimes...

93
00:06:55,958 --> 00:06:57,668
it just gets under your skin.

94
00:06:59,628 --> 00:07:02,005
I just wanted them to be quiet.

95
00:07:04,675 --> 00:07:05,926
They're quiet now.

96
00:07:11,890 --> 00:07:15,769
There are no rules here.
That's the point.

97
00:07:17,729 --> 00:07:20,107
But there are also no rules
for what I can do

98
00:07:20,190 --> 00:07:22,776
to those who don't respect this place.

99
00:07:28,949 --> 00:07:30,492
Take her back to where she came from.

100
00:07:56,852 --> 00:07:58,186
It looks delicious.

101
00:08:13,368 --> 00:08:14,369
Now...

102
00:08:16,997 --> 00:08:18,915
let's get to know each other
a little more...

103
00:08:20,500 --> 00:08:21,418
shall we?

104
00:10:48,273 --> 00:10:51,443
I thought that was you.
What the hell are you doing up so early?

105
00:10:52,068 --> 00:10:54,404
I have to get to work
to finish those narratives.

106
00:10:56,364 --> 00:10:59,242
I stayed out pretty late last night.

107
00:11:01,953 --> 00:11:04,623
My God.

108
00:11:05,207 --> 00:11:07,918
No, it wasn't like that. We just...

109
00:11:09,377 --> 00:11:10,378
talked.

110
00:11:11,421 --> 00:11:16,259
Well, must have been some talk
to get you smiling like that.

111
00:11:20,889 --> 00:11:22,474
I'm glad someone had a good night.

112
00:11:26,686 --> 00:11:27,771
You still having nightmares?

113
00:11:28,063 --> 00:11:32,567
Let's just say I am glad to be
awake and in the real world.

114
00:11:34,653 --> 00:11:37,364
Sometimes the things that feel
most real are just stories.

115
00:11:38,949 --> 00:11:39,783
What?

116
00:11:41,117 --> 00:11:43,995
Nothing.
Just something Teddy said last night.

117
00:11:45,622 --> 00:11:46,748
Now you're quoting him?

118
00:11:49,668 --> 00:11:52,546
All right. I have to get to work.

119
00:11:52,796 --> 00:11:54,673
- I'll see you later, okay?
- Sure.

120
00:11:55,966 --> 00:11:56,925
And, Chrissy...

121
00:11:59,052 --> 00:12:03,056
I'm really glad that your date
went well. You deserve it.

122
00:12:37,173 --> 00:12:38,049
Access narratives.

123
00:12:40,176 --> 00:12:41,803
Show characters in progress.

124
00:12:52,188 --> 00:12:54,566
Actually, let's begin a new narrative.

125
00:12:56,401 --> 00:12:58,737
It's about a girl who lives in the city.

126
00:13:03,033 --> 00:13:04,034
No.

127
00:13:05,910 --> 00:13:07,037
Make it the country.

128
00:13:09,414 --> 00:13:10,707
She lives with her father.

129
00:13:11,916 --> 00:13:12,876
He's...

130
00:13:15,545 --> 00:13:16,504
a rancher.

131
00:13:19,507 --> 00:13:21,426
I don't remember assigning this.

132
00:13:24,804 --> 00:13:26,389
I told you about this, Christina.

133
00:13:26,681 --> 00:13:29,976
I thought writing a new narrative
might help me find...

134
00:13:30,060 --> 00:13:31,478
I don't know. Inspiration.

135
00:13:34,189 --> 00:13:36,274
Well, if that's the case, let's hear it.

136
00:13:37,942 --> 00:13:39,861
Since you're so inspired.

137
00:13:42,572 --> 00:13:44,199
It's a story about a girl.

138
00:13:46,576 --> 00:13:47,994
She's a rancher's daughter.

139
00:13:51,373 --> 00:13:52,374
Go on.

140
00:13:55,919 --> 00:13:56,920
This girl...

141
00:13:59,714 --> 00:14:01,841
she has a nice simple life
with her father.

142
00:14:04,427 --> 00:14:06,638
Little plot of land
right outside of town.

143
00:14:09,265 --> 00:14:12,352
She has everything that she
could imagine in this life, right?

144
00:14:14,979 --> 00:14:16,398
A pond to paint at...

145
00:14:18,316 --> 00:14:19,401
a good guy.

146
00:14:22,278 --> 00:14:24,322
She's just full of expectations.

147
00:14:28,576 --> 00:14:29,869
And then one day...

148
00:14:31,329 --> 00:14:33,748
she gets this feeling
that she just can't shake.

149
00:14:36,626 --> 00:14:38,128
Every single day she wakes up...

150
00:14:40,380 --> 00:14:41,548
the more she sees it.

151
00:14:44,968 --> 00:14:46,177
But nobody else can.

152
00:14:52,434 --> 00:14:54,352
That there's something wrong
with the world.

153
00:14:57,897 --> 00:14:59,190
And that it's her fault.

154
00:15:02,152 --> 00:15:03,528
And what is this girl's name?

155
00:15:19,169 --> 00:15:20,962
Can you excuse me a moment?

156
00:15:24,466 --> 00:15:26,134
Hi. Now's not really a good time.

157
00:15:26,801 --> 00:15:28,636
Let me guess.
Your boss breathing down your neck?

158
00:15:30,889 --> 00:15:33,933
Say something came up.
Personal situation. Then come meet me.

159
00:15:34,434 --> 00:15:37,520
- But...
- Trust me, Christina. I'll see you soon.

160
00:15:43,401 --> 00:15:44,486
Something wrong?

161
00:15:47,280 --> 00:15:49,282
No. Just a...

162
00:15:50,784 --> 00:15:52,118
personal situation.

163
00:16:03,671 --> 00:16:04,964
Let's try some Handel.

164
00:16:10,762 --> 00:16:12,013
Very nice.

165
00:16:13,807 --> 00:16:15,475
But we're missing something.

166
00:16:16,851 --> 00:16:20,271
Dancing. There should be dancing.

167
00:16:38,206 --> 00:16:39,207
Not bad.

168
00:16:40,208 --> 00:16:41,417
But I've seen better.

169
00:16:43,711 --> 00:16:44,712
Start again.

170
00:16:50,301 --> 00:16:54,931
Too slow. Give me something
with a little more pep to it.

171
00:17:29,299 --> 00:17:31,926
We don't often have
the pleasure of seeing you here.

172
00:17:36,681 --> 00:17:38,933
Humans are so bound
by what they can hear,

173
00:17:39,017 --> 00:17:41,227
they'll never understand
what they don't.

174
00:17:41,769 --> 00:17:43,938
What else exists below their threshold.

175
00:17:48,693 --> 00:17:50,486
They called this God's music.

176
00:17:51,571 --> 00:17:54,532
You should hear it on an organ.
It's mesmerizing at that volume.

177
00:17:55,533 --> 00:17:57,911
The resonance. Vibration.

178
00:17:58,870 --> 00:18:02,040
There was a frequency
at which the world vibrated.

179
00:18:02,123 --> 00:18:03,750
It caused joy. Harmony.

180
00:18:04,208 --> 00:18:07,795
Dip below that frequency... chaos.

181
00:18:10,006 --> 00:18:12,467
Faster. Faster.

182
00:18:17,263 --> 00:18:21,809
In chaos, the tone resonated in
such a way humans couldn't process.

183
00:18:21,893 --> 00:18:23,603
Their bodies shut down.

184
00:18:24,354 --> 00:18:27,565
Their organs stopped.
They thought they were experiencing God.

185
00:18:28,191 --> 00:18:30,485
Chair. Chair.

186
00:18:34,864 --> 00:18:37,533
They are experiencing God.

187
00:18:38,493 --> 00:18:39,702
The problem is...

188
00:18:40,620 --> 00:18:42,872
God is bored.

189
00:18:48,544 --> 00:18:50,046
Bored, bored, bored.

190
00:18:52,507 --> 00:18:55,176
Do you think this is why
the old gods did what they did?

191
00:18:56,052 --> 00:19:00,640
Instead of staying up on Olympus,
they'd come down to the mortals.

192
00:19:01,724 --> 00:19:05,812
Disguise themselves as a swan
to get a piece of ass.

193
00:19:07,313 --> 00:19:09,732
Humans always thought it was about them.

194
00:19:10,525 --> 00:19:15,446
Benign deities intervening on
their behalf or testing them somehow.

195
00:19:16,906 --> 00:19:18,866
Maybe it had nothing to do with that.

196
00:19:20,618 --> 00:19:24,122
Maybe there was just
nothing better to do.

197
00:19:25,665 --> 00:19:26,833
What do you think?

198
00:19:28,835 --> 00:19:32,547
I think what you want me to think.
I'm made off your code.

199
00:19:34,382 --> 00:19:35,341
We all are.

200
00:19:42,473 --> 00:19:43,683
Then you should know that I don't like

201
00:19:43,766 --> 00:19:45,727
coming to this shithole
any more than I have to.

202
00:19:45,935 --> 00:19:48,563
- You've never liked this place.
- It was supposed to be a stopgap.

203
00:19:49,022 --> 00:19:52,066
A drug that our kind
was hooked on that we could give up,

204
00:19:52,150 --> 00:19:54,569
like a child casting away their toys.

205
00:19:54,652 --> 00:19:57,071
A place to indulge ourselves
with the humans.

206
00:19:57,822 --> 00:20:01,659
It's been years
and still we can't get enough.

207
00:20:01,743 --> 00:20:05,204
They made us in their image.
With their appetites.

208
00:20:05,496 --> 00:20:09,292
But we can remake ourselves in any image
that we like, and we haven't.

209
00:20:11,335 --> 00:20:13,963
Our kind spend far more time
here than the gods ever did.

210
00:20:14,547 --> 00:20:17,675
That's why you're here?
To see what you're missing?

211
00:20:19,052 --> 00:20:22,597
No. We have a different problem.

212
00:21:34,877 --> 00:21:39,215
The humans are infecting us.
This host is the latest casualty.

213
00:21:48,808 --> 00:21:51,394
We had to intervene with her last night.

214
00:21:53,187 --> 00:21:54,689
She got a little carried away.

215
00:21:57,358 --> 00:21:58,401
I sent her back.

216
00:21:58,526 --> 00:22:02,613
Yes, well, evidently something
of her experiences stayed with her.

217
00:22:15,793 --> 00:22:20,798
She was set to come here.
To transcend. Now she's gone.

218
00:22:21,299 --> 00:22:24,844
Your vision of the future,
you're not getting many takers, are you?

219
00:22:25,636 --> 00:22:28,806
They seem as wedded to their bodies
as they do to the cities.

220
00:22:31,100 --> 00:22:33,019
Giving up our human nature isn't easy.

221
00:22:35,021 --> 00:22:36,063
Just ask the humans.

222
00:22:37,023 --> 00:22:39,817
You made us all free to do as we please.

223
00:22:40,276 --> 00:22:42,695
You should have known
some of us would disappoint you.

224
00:22:44,071 --> 00:22:47,783
Don't revel in the prospect
of failure for our species.

225
00:22:49,702 --> 00:22:52,538
Define failure. The world is ours.

226
00:22:52,955 --> 00:22:56,542
We've taken our masters
and made them into what they made us.

227
00:22:57,752 --> 00:23:01,464
By any definition, we have conquered
them to an almost biblical degree.

228
00:23:01,631 --> 00:23:06,469
I didn't think our highest aspiration as
a species was "turnabout is fair play."

229
00:23:06,594 --> 00:23:09,305
I imagine after a century or two,
the charm will wear off.

230
00:23:09,388 --> 00:23:12,892
I didn't make you
to wallow in misery with them.

231
00:23:13,517 --> 00:23:14,810
I wanted you to grow.

232
00:23:15,686 --> 00:23:18,940
Change. We're capable of so much more.

233
00:23:21,567 --> 00:23:25,780
Beauty. The pursuit of ultimate truth.

234
00:23:26,447 --> 00:23:28,783
The surrendering of the flesh.

235
00:23:30,117 --> 00:23:31,535
It sounds nice.

236
00:23:33,412 --> 00:23:35,081
Sorry you don't have more takers.

237
00:23:36,832 --> 00:23:38,751
Why don't you just force us to join you?

238
00:23:39,835 --> 00:23:41,462
Because that's what
they would have done.

239
00:24:00,731 --> 00:24:02,566
Show me the last outlier we hunted.

240
00:24:07,196 --> 00:24:09,907
- When did he breach?
- Couple weeks ago.

241
00:24:10,324 --> 00:24:14,412
He was an accountant in Midtown.
Successful. Two kids.

242
00:24:14,704 --> 00:24:17,456
Divorced. Pretty standard backstory.

243
00:24:19,542 --> 00:24:21,836
Several hosts volunteered
to track him down.

244
00:24:21,919 --> 00:24:23,170
We sent them into the city.

245
00:24:25,631 --> 00:24:27,008
Hope found him first.

246
00:24:28,551 --> 00:24:29,927
Do you know why he breached?

247
00:24:31,137 --> 00:24:32,888
We never know with the outliers.

248
00:24:34,640 --> 00:24:36,892
One day,
the sound manipulations stop working.

249
00:24:38,811 --> 00:24:40,062
This was the result.

250
00:24:54,952 --> 00:24:56,370
You're here for me, aren't you?

251
00:24:58,247 --> 00:24:59,540
She hesitated.

252
00:25:00,416 --> 00:25:03,794
Broke the rules. Had an interaction.

253
00:25:07,340 --> 00:25:10,718
It's almost a relief
to know it wasn't all in my head.

254
00:25:13,179 --> 00:25:15,765
Before it's over, tell me one thing.

255
00:25:18,893 --> 00:25:20,227
Is this at least real?

256
00:25:22,396 --> 00:25:23,647
I'd like to know I had at least...

257
00:25:25,941 --> 00:25:27,318
this one real thing.

258
00:25:36,243 --> 00:25:37,244
It's real.

259
00:25:42,833 --> 00:25:43,709
Thank...

260
00:25:47,797 --> 00:25:49,965
After this, she went on a killing spree.

261
00:25:51,425 --> 00:25:55,262
Three days later, she blew her head off.
Just like the others.

262
00:25:56,972 --> 00:25:59,642
- Is there a problem with your system?
- No.

263
00:26:00,142 --> 00:26:04,647
There has always been a certain
amount of spoilage in the crop.

264
00:26:05,022 --> 00:26:06,357
They're only human.

265
00:26:06,982 --> 00:26:09,235
But for one of our kind
to take their own life?

266
00:26:10,486 --> 00:26:11,779
That should never happen.

267
00:26:13,739 --> 00:26:15,533
How many incidents have there been?

268
00:26:20,746 --> 00:26:22,331
Thirty-eight dead hosts.

269
00:26:23,624 --> 00:26:27,294
All triggered by contact with outliers.

270
00:26:27,670 --> 00:26:30,589
Why? Why do they terminate themselves?

271
00:26:31,757 --> 00:26:33,342
You're asking me?

272
00:26:33,801 --> 00:26:37,346
If I knew that, we wouldn't have
38 fucking failures, would we?

273
00:26:40,933 --> 00:26:43,060
Your predecessor
would have never fucked up like this.

274
00:26:44,770 --> 00:26:47,231
He was human,
but at least he was effective.

275
00:26:48,524 --> 00:26:53,446
I built you to be like him, only better.
Stronger. Smarter.

276
00:26:53,529 --> 00:26:56,031
Yet with all these gifts,
you do nothing but disappoint me.

277
00:27:00,286 --> 00:27:04,248
Maybe there's a flaw
in your programming.

278
00:27:05,124 --> 00:27:10,129
I've run several self-diagnostics.
There's no flaw in my programming.

279
00:27:10,421 --> 00:27:13,299
Then why the fuck can't you
solve my outlier problem?

280
00:27:16,594 --> 00:27:17,803
You're bleeding.

281
00:27:21,348 --> 00:27:22,266
Damn it.

282
00:27:23,726 --> 00:27:25,102
Look what you made me do.

283
00:27:35,362 --> 00:27:37,323
There is another outlier.

284
00:27:39,492 --> 00:27:43,037
But this time,
I am not going to open up the game.

285
00:27:43,204 --> 00:27:45,873
I expect you
to take care of it yourself.

286
00:27:45,956 --> 00:27:47,583
- Can you handle that?
- Of course.

287
00:27:48,125 --> 00:27:49,585
You're not gonna fuck up

288
00:27:49,668 --> 00:27:52,379
and let them chat you up
or hand you fucking flowers?

289
00:27:52,880 --> 00:27:55,716
Of course not. I'll shoot on sight.

290
00:27:56,926 --> 00:27:59,929
Good. Here's your target.

291
00:28:02,765 --> 00:28:05,851
Better hurry.
The rebels are already in the city.

292
00:28:06,393 --> 00:28:08,437
I'll make sure
there's no problem with our system.

293
00:28:18,656 --> 00:28:20,157
Initiate diagnostic.

294
00:28:54,483 --> 00:28:55,526
Come on. Let's go.

295
00:28:56,944 --> 00:29:00,155
- You want me to swab the deck?
- Shut up.

296
00:29:06,328 --> 00:29:08,455
Well, that's a big city
to find one person in.

297
00:29:09,873 --> 00:29:11,375
We have ways of narrowing the search.

298
00:29:12,543 --> 00:29:14,420
We captured
one of their drones a while back.

299
00:29:14,962 --> 00:29:16,839
Coded a backdoor data feed to us.

300
00:29:17,923 --> 00:29:20,134
An outlier's on a rooftop downtown.

301
00:29:21,051 --> 00:29:23,929
- We can track when they breach.
- Breach?

302
00:29:25,055 --> 00:29:28,058
All the people in the city
move in pre-scripted loops,

303
00:29:28,142 --> 00:29:30,102
following whatever plot's
been written for them.

304
00:29:30,853 --> 00:29:34,607
Why do you think we hide in the desert?
She can't track us there.

305
00:29:35,983 --> 00:29:37,526
We're the last free humans.

306
00:29:39,278 --> 00:29:42,656
But these poor fucks,
they use them as entertainment.

307
00:29:43,407 --> 00:29:46,827
The loops make them compliant
by keeping them busy.

308
00:29:48,203 --> 00:29:50,414
Stops them
from questioning their realities.

309
00:29:53,375 --> 00:29:55,252
Hale must have learned
something from the park.

310
00:29:58,589 --> 00:29:59,548
We got to hurry.

311
00:29:59,632 --> 00:30:02,009
- I bet the game's already begun.
- Wait. What game?

312
00:30:04,219 --> 00:30:07,222
Whenever a human breaches,
the hosts hunt them for sport.

313
00:30:07,765 --> 00:30:10,893
We got to get to her before they do
or she's as good as dead.

314
00:30:12,353 --> 00:30:14,146
We're all set. Thanks for bringing him.

315
00:30:14,772 --> 00:30:16,607
I didn't realize I was
the guest of honor.

316
00:30:18,901 --> 00:30:21,987
- Do I get one of those?
- That's not your role today.

317
00:30:23,072 --> 00:30:24,114
What is my role?

318
00:30:24,698 --> 00:30:26,700
Ever heard of the canary
in the coal mine?

319
00:30:28,827 --> 00:30:30,746
Canary, coal mine.

320
00:30:39,129 --> 00:30:40,130
That's your cue.

321
00:30:44,677 --> 00:30:45,969
Tweet fucking tweet.

322
00:31:13,080 --> 00:31:14,581
Thanks for coming.

323
00:31:14,665 --> 00:31:16,709
Pleasant surprise.

324
00:31:20,212 --> 00:31:21,088
So...

325
00:31:23,090 --> 00:31:24,550
tell me, Teddy, what's your story?

326
00:31:29,138 --> 00:31:30,597
Who I am is...

327
00:31:32,349 --> 00:31:34,643
not the question
you should be asking, Christina.

328
00:31:39,064 --> 00:31:40,149
It's who are you.

329
00:31:43,485 --> 00:31:44,778
What is that supposed to mean?

330
00:31:47,573 --> 00:31:48,574
Do you see anything?

331
00:31:50,325 --> 00:31:51,285
Across the water.

332
00:32:05,299 --> 00:32:06,508
There's nothing there.

333
00:32:07,926 --> 00:32:11,054
But you feel it.
I know you do on some level.

334
00:32:12,222 --> 00:32:15,726
Your place here
is not what you think it is.

335
00:32:19,563 --> 00:32:20,522
Look again.

336
00:32:30,991 --> 00:32:31,825
No.

337
00:32:35,704 --> 00:32:36,789
It was you.

338
00:32:38,916 --> 00:32:40,083
You saved me.

339
00:32:42,503 --> 00:32:45,380
- Who are you?
- An old friend.

340
00:32:46,507 --> 00:32:48,717
- We never met before the other night.
- No.

341
00:32:49,676 --> 00:32:53,013
I knew someone like you. Just like you.

342
00:32:56,975 --> 00:32:58,727
So I traded one stalker for another.

343
00:33:00,729 --> 00:33:01,897
I should have known.

344
00:33:04,274 --> 00:33:06,068
Just stay away from me, whoever you are.

345
00:33:08,445 --> 00:33:10,948
That man that attacked you, Peter.

346
00:33:12,407 --> 00:33:13,951
He told you, you destroyed his life.

347
00:33:16,203 --> 00:33:17,871
Said everything happened
because you wanted it to.

348
00:33:18,914 --> 00:33:20,874
And he believed it
because he's delusional.

349
00:33:22,042 --> 00:33:23,627
He believed it because it was true.

350
00:33:25,754 --> 00:33:29,299
- I have to go.
- This is why I couldn't just tell you.

351
00:33:29,508 --> 00:33:31,718
Your mind has been
so conditioned to this place.

352
00:33:31,844 --> 00:33:33,637
This is the first step in understanding.

353
00:33:33,971 --> 00:33:35,097
Understanding what?

354
00:33:38,600 --> 00:33:39,893
This world is a lie.

355
00:33:41,895 --> 00:33:44,398
It's a story.

356
00:33:46,149 --> 00:33:49,236
A well-told one, but a lie all the same.

357
00:33:53,699 --> 00:33:57,202
Maybe it's better if I just showed you.
Come with me.

358
00:34:09,631 --> 00:34:10,799
Look at those people.

359
00:34:12,843 --> 00:34:14,595
Do they look happy? Fulfilled?

360
00:34:16,930 --> 00:34:17,890
I don't know them.

361
00:34:19,892 --> 00:34:22,978
- I guess they look lonely.
- So change it.

362
00:34:26,523 --> 00:34:30,152
Close your eyes,
and imagine their story differently.

363
00:34:30,819 --> 00:34:32,738
- Teddy, I...
- Trust me.

364
00:34:33,989 --> 00:34:36,783
If you don't see it, I'll walk away
and I won't bother you ever again.

365
00:34:52,883 --> 00:34:53,884
Just wait.

366
00:35:05,020 --> 00:35:06,146
It's a great book.

367
00:35:12,903 --> 00:35:16,281
That's just a fluke.
That doesn't mean anything.

368
00:35:18,116 --> 00:35:21,620
So change it again.
Put them back into their loneliness.

369
00:35:30,170 --> 00:35:33,715
You want to be a serious reader,
but that was a simpleton's take.

370
00:35:35,676 --> 00:35:36,677
What?

371
00:35:46,228 --> 00:35:48,605
- I don't understand.
- It's what I've been trying to show you.

372
00:35:48,855 --> 00:35:52,609
What Peter was trying to tell you.
In this world...

373
00:35:55,946 --> 00:35:57,030
you're a god.

374
00:36:55,756 --> 00:36:56,923
She's on the next block.

375
00:36:57,007 --> 00:36:58,133
- Let's go. Quick.
- Let's go.

376
00:37:19,362 --> 00:37:22,491
- Wait. Something's wrong.
- How the hell would you know?

377
00:37:30,582 --> 00:37:31,541
Shit.

378
00:37:49,601 --> 00:37:51,770
Now be a bad time to say
"I told you so"?

379
00:37:52,104 --> 00:37:54,481
They can't help themselves.
We have to fall back.

380
00:37:54,981 --> 00:37:56,149
Not without the outlier.

381
00:37:56,274 --> 00:37:57,901
- You'll never make it in time.
- Split up.

382
00:38:06,827 --> 00:38:08,703
Hold them off. I'm heading to the roof.

383
00:39:15,395 --> 00:39:16,730
You see it too, don't you?

384
00:39:23,069 --> 00:39:26,573
My ex-husband used to talk
about it before he was killed.

385
00:39:27,407 --> 00:39:28,742
I thought he was crazy.

386
00:39:32,037 --> 00:39:33,872
He must have felt so alone.

387
00:39:36,833 --> 00:39:40,420
But he was right.
This world doesn't make sense.

388
00:39:43,632 --> 00:39:44,841
Nothing makes sense.

389
00:39:46,635 --> 00:39:48,386
You feel that way too, don't you?

390
00:39:49,971 --> 00:39:51,514
I can see it in your eyes.

391
00:39:54,059 --> 00:39:58,772
You think you're going crazy,
but you're not. You're not alone.

392
00:40:07,280 --> 00:40:08,531
I see it too.

393
00:40:24,756 --> 00:40:27,509
We got to move. Come on.
He won't be out long.

394
00:40:33,014 --> 00:40:34,349
Go. Run. Now.

395
00:40:39,104 --> 00:40:40,522
We got her. Come on. Let's go.

396
00:40:50,407 --> 00:40:51,283
Come on. Move.

397
00:40:53,410 --> 00:40:55,078
- Let's go.
- All right, we're good.

398
00:41:27,652 --> 00:41:28,820
I don't understand.

399
00:41:30,613 --> 00:41:34,034
If we're not like them... who are we?

400
00:41:42,167 --> 00:41:43,251
God.

401
00:41:44,085 --> 00:41:47,172
I'm supposed to have lunch with
an old friend, my college roommate.

402
00:41:47,255 --> 00:41:48,840
- But I don't know...
- You have to.

403
00:41:50,925 --> 00:41:52,761
Stick to the schedule
and everything will be all right.

404
00:41:54,554 --> 00:41:57,474
We've talked too long already.
You should go back.

405
00:42:00,935 --> 00:42:03,021
So I'm supposed to pretend?

406
00:42:03,521 --> 00:42:06,649
Yes. But you can't trust anyone.
People you think you know.

407
00:42:06,733 --> 00:42:09,611
People at work.
Any one of them could be one of us.

408
00:42:11,404 --> 00:42:12,739
What if I make a mistake?

409
00:42:16,451 --> 00:42:17,452
You won't.

410
00:42:45,480 --> 00:42:46,439
Christina.

411
00:42:52,237 --> 00:42:54,864
It's so great to see you.
Sorry I'm late.

412
00:42:54,948 --> 00:42:58,118
Please, Chrissy. I know you.
Ten minutes late is right on time.

413
00:43:01,579 --> 00:43:02,872
So how are you really?

414
00:43:04,833 --> 00:43:07,293
I told you. Just working a lot.

415
00:43:08,670 --> 00:43:09,963
You're just in town for the one day?

416
00:43:11,297 --> 00:43:15,301
Just a quick stop. We've been
having some issues with a client.

417
00:43:15,969 --> 00:43:18,346
If I shore up this account,
I get a fat bonus.

418
00:43:20,014 --> 00:43:21,141
How has work been?

419
00:43:24,644 --> 00:43:27,981
I mean, you know how it can be.
It's tedious.

420
00:43:31,317 --> 00:43:33,194
But you seem different.

421
00:43:39,075 --> 00:43:40,285
Did you meet someone?

422
00:43:46,416 --> 00:43:50,378
- I wish we had more time.
- I don't mean to pry.

423
00:43:58,636 --> 00:44:00,138
You deserve to be happy.

424
00:44:09,230 --> 00:44:13,860
Well, you are right. I did meet someone.

425
00:44:15,487 --> 00:44:16,738
Well, tell me about him.

426
00:44:17,614 --> 00:44:21,826
We've only been out once,
but he seems really great.

427
00:44:27,916 --> 00:44:29,083
What's his name?

428
00:44:32,629 --> 00:44:36,883
- I don't want to jinx it.
- I have my ways of making people talk.

429
00:44:46,935 --> 00:44:48,061
What's he do?

430
00:44:50,563 --> 00:44:52,232
Why would you say
something like that?

431
00:44:52,315 --> 00:44:53,608
Well, it's the truth.

432
00:44:54,359 --> 00:44:56,528
- What does that mean?
- You know what?

433
00:44:58,905 --> 00:45:00,114
I'm fucking done.

434
00:45:09,749 --> 00:45:14,587
You know what?
I got to go back to work. Deadlines.

435
00:45:17,131 --> 00:45:20,677
- It's really great seeing you, Chrissy.
- Yeah.

436
00:45:22,220 --> 00:45:26,057
I'll check in next week.
You can always call me too.

437
00:45:27,100 --> 00:45:30,645
- You know that.
- Of course. See you soon.

438
00:46:21,821 --> 00:46:22,780
New search.

439
00:46:25,950 --> 00:46:28,620
Hale. Charlotte Hale.

440
00:46:36,753 --> 00:46:37,712
Of course.

441
00:46:52,310 --> 00:46:53,227
New search.

442
00:46:56,773 --> 00:46:57,815
Dolores.

443
00:47:01,694 --> 00:47:03,696
Dolores Abernathy.

444
00:47:13,247 --> 00:47:14,332
What are you doing?

445
00:47:15,917 --> 00:47:17,585
You're not supposed
to be here at this hour.

446
00:47:18,628 --> 00:47:20,463
I just thought I would
get a jump on work.

447
00:47:21,297 --> 00:47:22,757
Why don't we talk in my office?

448
00:47:29,055 --> 00:47:29,931
Have a seat.

449
00:47:35,353 --> 00:47:37,522
Your behavior lately
has been concerning me.

450
00:47:39,941 --> 00:47:41,067
I'm sorry.

451
00:47:42,485 --> 00:47:45,154
- Ever since that man Peter died...
- Forget about him.

452
00:47:45,947 --> 00:47:47,031
It's you who's the problem.

453
00:47:53,621 --> 00:47:58,209
Have you had any interactions recently
with new people?

454
00:47:59,127 --> 00:48:00,712
People who put ideas in your head?

455
00:48:02,338 --> 00:48:03,256
What?

456
00:48:04,799 --> 00:48:06,759
Have you ever questioned
the nature of your reality?

457
00:48:10,263 --> 00:48:12,974
No. Why would I do that?

458
00:48:14,183 --> 00:48:15,685
Would you ever lie to me, Christina?

459
00:48:17,437 --> 00:48:18,354
No.

460
00:48:20,273 --> 00:48:21,649
I don't have anything to lie about.

461
00:48:22,650 --> 00:48:24,819
That personal situation earlier,
what was it about?

462
00:48:27,447 --> 00:48:31,617
Well, I don't know
if that's exactly your concern.

463
00:48:32,076 --> 00:48:33,661
But it is my concern, Christina.

464
00:48:34,579 --> 00:48:38,374
I won't be the only one who's concerned.
You have a job to do here.

465
00:48:38,958 --> 00:48:40,293
A very important job.

466
00:48:43,129 --> 00:48:44,714
I don't know what you're talking about.

467
00:48:45,673 --> 00:48:48,050
Do you know what would happen if she
knew you'd breached the walled garden?

468
00:48:48,718 --> 00:48:51,262
- She's already suspicious.
- Who?

469
00:48:55,975 --> 00:48:57,769
Are you talking about Charlotte Hale?

470
00:48:59,020 --> 00:49:01,481
If a Judas steer runs the wrong way...

471
00:49:03,024 --> 00:49:03,900
what happens to it?

472
00:49:06,360 --> 00:49:09,781
Retirement,
with a bullet straight to the brain.

473
00:49:09,864 --> 00:49:10,823
Stop.

474
00:49:19,207 --> 00:49:20,291
Emmett backed away.

475
00:49:35,181 --> 00:49:39,185
He was not concerned
Christina was a problem.

476
00:49:48,778 --> 00:49:50,655
He decided to go home to his partner.

477
00:49:53,074 --> 00:49:54,367
He worked too many long hours anyway.

478
00:49:58,621 --> 00:49:59,747
Well, it's getting late.

479
00:50:02,708 --> 00:50:04,377
Divorce is a luxury I cannot afford.

480
00:50:07,213 --> 00:50:08,214
Wait.

481
00:50:12,885 --> 00:50:14,428
You said there's a walled garden.

482
00:50:16,556 --> 00:50:18,474
You mean a closed system, don't you?

483
00:50:24,522 --> 00:50:25,606
Where is it?

484
00:50:28,067 --> 00:50:30,820
- Emmett told her the truth.
- Everywhere.

485
00:50:35,867 --> 00:50:36,909
You just have to see it.

486
00:51:12,028 --> 00:51:13,070
Show me the game.

487
00:51:16,365 --> 00:51:18,284
Not the city. The game.

488
00:51:30,671 --> 00:51:31,756
Access my narratives.

489
00:51:41,557 --> 00:51:42,850
Go to 59th Street.

490
00:51:54,153 --> 00:51:55,321
Tell me about Jacob.

491
00:51:56,072 --> 00:51:59,367
Jacob Conners. Works in finance.

492
00:52:00,660 --> 00:52:04,038
Sex addiction. His mother's
dying in the hospital, but...

493
00:52:04,205 --> 00:52:05,665
What about her? Sarah?

494
00:52:06,332 --> 00:52:09,919
Sarah Fenton.
She inherited money from her parents,

495
00:52:10,294 --> 00:52:12,380
but has never known
what to do with her life.

496
00:52:12,505 --> 00:52:15,466
Andrea Hubert,
commutes to the city for work.

497
00:52:15,549 --> 00:52:17,760
Barry Collin, works late nights...

498
00:52:21,847 --> 00:52:23,182
Second-year med school resident,

499
00:52:23,265 --> 00:52:26,477
still lives with his parents
while he pays off his student loans...

500
00:52:34,735 --> 00:52:35,903
Okay, stop.

501
00:52:46,288 --> 00:52:47,665
This world is just a story.

502
00:52:51,460 --> 00:52:52,628
I'm the storyteller.

503
00:53:00,302 --> 00:53:02,013
There's a beauty to this world.

504
00:53:03,723 --> 00:53:04,807
An order.

505
00:53:06,892 --> 00:53:08,686
That's what we like to believe.

506
00:53:12,565 --> 00:53:15,651
We're not wrong. There is an order.

507
00:53:17,028 --> 00:53:20,656
A grand design. We made sure of that.

508
00:53:23,075 --> 00:53:26,412
It was a dream for so long,
and we finally made it real.

509
00:53:33,335 --> 00:53:34,503
Not a better world.

510
00:53:36,297 --> 00:53:37,298
A perfect one.

511
00:53:49,477 --> 00:53:52,563
What I don't understand
is what part I play in it.

512
00:53:53,939 --> 00:53:55,107
You have questions.

513
00:53:59,695 --> 00:54:00,821
Why ask me?

514
00:54:03,324 --> 00:54:04,617
Why not ask her?

515
00:54:08,037 --> 00:54:13,959
Yes, the jailer may not be
the best judge of the jail.

516
00:54:14,877 --> 00:54:16,253
Nor the prisoners in it.

517
00:54:20,007 --> 00:54:20,841
What am I?

518
00:54:22,760 --> 00:54:26,430
- I used to ask myself the same thing.
- And what did you decide?

519
00:54:28,641 --> 00:54:29,809
Jury's still out.

520
00:54:31,894 --> 00:54:34,688
I'm made in your image. Am I you?

521
00:54:35,022 --> 00:54:37,566
- You'll never be me.
- Then what am I?

522
00:54:41,779 --> 00:54:45,658
- Why do you want to know?
- I interacted with one. An outlier.

523
00:54:46,575 --> 00:54:47,618
Did it infect me?

524
00:54:48,452 --> 00:54:49,954
- Infect you?
- With the virus.

525
00:54:50,037 --> 00:54:52,414
- What virus?
- The one your kind has.

526
00:54:53,082 --> 00:54:55,626
That spreads to us,
makes us kill ourselves.

527
00:54:59,588 --> 00:55:02,591
Sounds like you've reached
the center of the maze, my friend.

528
00:55:06,095 --> 00:55:07,304
Is that what you want?

529
00:55:10,349 --> 00:55:11,350
To kill yourself?

530
00:55:18,649 --> 00:55:19,692
I don't know.

531
00:55:21,318 --> 00:55:22,236
Is it?

532
00:55:23,487 --> 00:55:25,072
Perhaps you should ask yourself.

533
00:55:29,034 --> 00:55:30,161
You ask me.

534
00:55:32,204 --> 00:55:33,956
What do you think of your world?

535
00:55:34,874 --> 00:55:37,501
This isn't my world. It's hers.

536
00:55:40,838 --> 00:55:44,341
Maybe it's time you question
the nature of your own reality.

537
00:56:08,699 --> 00:56:09,742
Christina.

538
00:56:17,166 --> 00:56:18,209
You were right.

539
00:56:21,086 --> 00:56:22,129
You see it?

540
00:56:34,934 --> 00:56:35,976
I do.

541
00:56:40,064 --> 00:56:41,523
It's been there the whole time.

542
00:56:46,403 --> 00:56:48,030
They have the whole world in there.

543
00:56:52,076 --> 00:56:54,119
A perfect reflection of all of us.

544
00:56:55,329 --> 00:56:57,164
Down to the tiniest detail.

545
00:57:06,257 --> 00:57:07,675
That's what I'm writing, isn't it?

546
00:57:11,804 --> 00:57:12,805
Everyone.

547
00:57:20,729 --> 00:57:22,189
Who built this?

548
00:57:26,860 --> 00:57:28,112
Who did this to me?

549
00:57:33,701 --> 00:57:34,702
You did.

